---
import PageSection from "../../components/PageSection.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { copy } from "../../lib/i18n";
import { webkitPatches } from "../../lib/webkit-patches";

const lang = "en";
const text = copy[lang];

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        item: "https://getlilo.app/",
        name: "Lilo Browser",
        position: 1,
      },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        item: "https://getlilo.app/webkit/",
        name: "Lilo and WebKit",
        position: 2,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://getlilo.app/webkit/#faq",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    inLanguage: "en",
    mainEntity: [],
    url: "https://getlilo.app/webkit/",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  lang={lang}
  title="Lilo and WebKit: the engine fixes Lilo sends upstream"
  description="Lilo is a Mac browser on WebKit, the engine Safari uses. When WebKit makes a page slow or broken, Lilo fixes the engine itself and sends each patch upstream."
  schema={schema}
  hero={{
    heading: text.webkitHeading,
    description: text.webkitIntroduction,
    tag: text.webkit,
    link: { href: "#patches", label: text.webkitHeroLink },
  }}
  closing={{
    heading: text.webkitClosingHeading,
    description: text.webkitClosingDescription,
  }}
>
  <PageSection id="patches">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="tag">Sent upstream</span>
      <h2>Fixes in WebKit itself.</h2>
      <p>
        Each one has a WebKit bug and a pull request, and leaves this list once
        it lands in WebKit.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feat-list">
      {webkitPatches.map((patch) => (
        <div>
          {patch.fix.en}{" "}
          <a href={`https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=${patch.bug}`}>
            Bug {patch.bug}
          </a>
          ,{" "}
          <a
            href={`https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/pull/${patch.pullRequest}`}
          >
            pull request #{patch.pullRequest}
          </a>
          .
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="method">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="tag">How we work</span>
      <h2>Measure, reduce, fix the engine.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="feat-list two">
      <div>
        <b>Measured on real pages.</b> We profile the sites people use most,
        compare them with Chrome and with the WebKit of macOS, then reduce each
        slowdown to a small page any WebKit developer can run.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Generic, never per site.</b> No hostname checks and no quirks for one
        site: a fix changes how the engine works, so every page gets it.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Upstream first.</b> Every patch goes to WebKit with its bug, a
        performance test and the numbers, for WebKit's own reviewers.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Never a requirement.</b> Lilo works fully on the WebKit of macOS. Its
        own WebKit build only brings fixes earlier, and runs only while it holds
        every security fix of your macOS.
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="faq" layout="faq">
    <div class="head" style="margin:0">
      <span class="tag">Questions</span>
      <h2>Lilo and its engine.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details>
        <summary>Is Lilo just a wrapper around WKWebView?</summary>
        <p>
          Lilo shows pages with WKWebView, the way Mac apps embed WebKit. Around
          it, Lilo is a browser of its own in Swift and AppKit: Spaces, Chrome
          extensions on WebKit's extension runtime with Swift handlers for the
          APIs it lacks, terminal tabs and an MCP server. And when the engine is
          the problem, Lilo fixes the engine.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Do I need anything to get these fixes?</summary>
        <p>
          No. Once a fix lands in WebKit, it ships with the next Safari and
          macOS updates, and Lilo runs on that same WebKit. Lilo's own WebKit
          build can bring the fixes sooner, and you can read
          <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/blob/main/docs/WEBKIT_FORK.md">
            how it stays safe
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Why fix WebKit instead of switching to Chromium?</summary>
        <p>
          WebKit is the engine already in macOS: it draws less power, follows
          every macOS release, and keeps Lilo a native Mac app. Fixing it helps
          Safari and every Mac app that shows the web, not only Lilo.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I report a slow page?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes.
          <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/issues">
            Open an issue
          </a>{" "}
          with the page and what you did. If the engine is the cause, the fix
          goes to WebKit.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </PageSection>
</Layout>
